# Symbolic differentiation as forward-chaining rules, following the
# Trigger / Decompose / Assemble / Connect architecture.
#
# Usage:
#   x ~ symvar
#   (x * x) diffby x
#   ((x * x) diffby x) = D    # repeatable result query; answers (x + x)
#
# The raw derivative is deliberately NOT the exposed result: the connect
# stage asserts an ordinary (D simplify D) request, symbolic-core
# answers it, and the simplified form is exposed under = -- the same
# cross-module cascade as MA1/MA2 in the arithmetic modules.
#
# Constant subterms are recognized via a positive containment recursion
# plus negation-as-failure (the primes-naf pattern). Numerals need no
# special rule: nothing derives (&n contains X), so they are constants
# by absence. The structural rules redundantly differentiate constant
# composites too; both paths simplify to the same &0, so = stays
# single-valued -- confluence through the simplifier, pinned in
# test_symbolic.cpp.
#
# Undeclared sorts yield NO answer, never a wrong one: the constancy
# rule is guarded by X ~ symvar, so a forgotten declaration results in
# silence instead of everything counting as constant. Operators outside
# {+, *, of} get no derivative -- partiality by absence.

.import symbolic-core

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Function-symbol derivatives: F hasderivative G means d F(u)/du = G(u).
# ln has a dedicated rule below -- its derivative 1/u is not of the form
# G(u) for a named symbol G.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
exp hasderivative exp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule DT0 -- Trigger
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffby X) => (T dstate X)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DD -- Decompose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) dstate X) => (U dstate X)
((U + V) dstate X) => (V dstate X)
((U * V) dstate X) => (U dstate X)
((U * V) dstate X) => (V dstate X)
((F of U) dstate X) => (U dstate X)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DO -- Occurrence: positive containment recursion, anchored on
# dstate so only relevant composites are ever considered.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(X ~ symvar) => (X contains X)

((U + V) dstate X, U contains X) => ((U + V) contains X)
((U + V) dstate X, V contains X) => ((U + V) contains X)
((U * V) dstate X, U contains X) => ((U * V) contains X)
((U * V) dstate X, V contains X) => ((U * V) contains X)
((F of U) dstate X, U contains X) => ((F of U) contains X)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DS -- Shape domain: the forms whose derivative this module knows.
#
# The constancy rule below is a NEGATION, and a negation is only as sound
# as the positive recursion it tests. `contains` walks exactly the forms
# the DD rules decompose; the TRIGGER, however, hands a dstate fact to
# WHATEVER was requested. Without this marker a term outside the
# vocabulary -- (x / y), or any foreign predicate -- fails the containment
# test for the trivial reason that no rule could ever have derived it, and
# was declared constant: a WRONG answer where the stdlib owes silence.
#
# So membership in the domain is stated positively, one rule per form,
# and the constancy rule is gated on it. This is the fourth contribution
# of the OPERATOR EXTENSION PROTOCOL: an operator that joins
# differentiation declares its shape here, exactly as it declares its
# decompose, containment and assemble rules (see symbolic-minus.zph and
# symbolic-pow.zph).
#
# Note the variable positions: a dstate fact is (TERM dstate VARIABLE), so
# the leaf rules must NOT be written as (X dstate X, X ~ symvar) -- that
# self-fact form only matches a leaf differentiated with respect to
# ITSELF, and every other leaf (the y in d(x*y)/dx) would lose its
# constancy answer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T dstate X, T ~ symvar)   => (T ddom T)
(T dstate X, T ~ symconst) => (T ddom T)
((A cons R) dstate X)      => ((A cons R) ddom (A cons R))

((U + V) dstate X)  => ((U + V) ddom (U + V))
((U * V) dstate X)  => ((U * V) ddom (U * V))
((F of U) dstate X) => ((F of U) ddom (F of U))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DB -- Base cases
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(X dstate X, X ~ symvar) => ((X wrt X) deriv &1)

# Anything of a KNOWN SHAPE not containing X is constant with respect to
# X. Deferred by stratification until the containment recursion is
# saturated -- the textbook rule, sound as written (see primes-naf).
(T dstate X, X ~ symvar, T ddom T, ¬(T contains X)) => ((T wrt X) deriv &0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DA -- Assemble: sum, product, chain. Consequences build the
# derivative terms as ordinary graph structure via fact().
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P,
 (V wrt X) deriv Q)
=> (((U + V) wrt X) deriv (P + Q))

((U * V) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P,
 (V wrt X) deriv Q)
=> (((U * V) wrt X) deriv ((P * V) + (U * Q)))

((F of U) dstate X,
 F hasderivative G,
 (U wrt X) deriv P)
=> (((F of U) wrt X) deriv ((G of U) * P))

((ln of U) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P)
=> (((ln of U) wrt X) deriv (P / U))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DC -- Connect: pipe the raw derivative through the simplifier
# (cross-module cascade), then expose the simplified form under =.
# Repeatable query: (T diffby X) = D
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffby X, (T wrt X) deriv D) => (D simplify D)

(T diffby X,
 (T wrt X) deriv D,
 (D simplify D) = S)
=> ((T diffby X) = S)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DL -- Iterated differentiation along a cons list of variables.
#
#   (T diffalong <x y>) = E     d/dy (d/dx T)  -- reading order, outermost
#                               cell first, as in <x y z> ~ polyring
#   (T diffalong <x x>) = E     the second derivative
#   (T diffalong nil)   = T     the empty list differentiates nothing
#
# No new machinery: each step is an ordinary diffby request whose = result
# feeds the next one -- results are inputs again, the property the whole
# stdlib is built on. A cons list is used rather than a fresh pair former
# because facts carry a SET of objects, so (T d X Y) could not distinguish
# (T d x y) from (T d y x); the list fixes the order.
#
# Partiality composes: if any step derives no result, the chain stays
# silent. Mixed partials in both orders reach the same answer node -- the
# Schwarz/Clairaut symmetry, observable as node identity rather than
# assumed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffalong nil) => ((T diffalong nil) = T)

(T diffalong (V cons R)) => (T diffby V)

(T diffalong (V cons R), (T diffby V) = D) => (D diffalong R)

(T diffalong (V cons R),
 (T diffby V) = D,
 (D diffalong R) = E)
=> ((T diffalong (V cons R)) = E)
